pmsr:ROP1740092924252535
        rdf:type                        "vstoi:ResponseOption" ;
        rdfs:comment                    "Neonatal Cubital fossa Median antecubital, cephalic and basilic veins - Median antecubital, cephalic and basilic veins are easy to hit and tend to last quite well if splinted properly.  These veins are the preferred sites for insertion of percutaneous central venous catheters.  These should be avoided unless absolutely necessary in any infant likely to need long term IV therapy. The median nerve and brachial artery are both in the same anatomical vicinity and therefore vulnerable to damage." ;
        rdfs:label                      "LOC ROP1740092924252535 - STD-STUDY-PMSR" ;
        hasco:hasDataAcquisition        "http://example.org/roundtrip/DA-DA-SOC-RESPONSE-OPTION-PMSR.csv" ;
        hasco:hasDataAcquisition        <http://example.org/roundtrip/DA-DA-SOC-RESPONSE-OPTION-PMSR.csv> ;
        hasco:hasDataFile               "http://example.org/roundtrip/DFL-DA-SOC-RESPONSE-OPTION-PMSR.csv" ;
        hasco:hasTimestamp              "2026-06-03T19:38:49.023Z" ;
        hasco:hasTotalRecordedMessages  "0" ;
        hasco:hasWebDocument            "https://www.safercare.vic.gov.au/best-practice-improvement/clinical-guidance/neonatal/peripheral-intravenous-iv-catheter-inserti" ;
        hasco:hascoType                 "vstoi:ResponseOption" ;
        vstoi:hasContent                "pmsr:/Neonatal Cubital fossa veins" ;
        vstoi:hasLanguage               "pmsr:/en" ;
        vstoi:hasStatus                 "UNPROCESSED" ;
        vstoi:hasVersion                "pmsr:/1" .

pmsr:ROP1740093084185465
        rdf:type                        "vstoi:ResponseOption" ;
        rdfs:comment                    "The saphenous vein runs just behind the medial aspect of the knee and is often visible behind the knee and as it curves around the top of the tibia.  Access is easy and lasts well if properly splinted. However, this vein is a good site for the insertion of percutaneous central venous catheters and should be avoided if possible in any infant likely to need long term IV access." ;
        rdfs:label                      "LOC ROP1740093084185465 - STD-STUDY-PMSR" ;
        hasco:hasDataAcquisition        "http://example.org/roundtrip/DA-DA-SOC-RESPONSE-OPTION-PMSR.csv" ;
        hasco:hasDataAcquisition        <http://example.org/roundtrip/DA-DA-SOC-RESPONSE-OPTION-PMSR.csv> ;
        hasco:hasDataFile               "http://example.org/roundtrip/DFL-DA-SOC-RESPONSE-OPTION-PMSR.csv" ;
        hasco:hasTimestamp              "2026-06-03T19:38:49.023Z" ;
        hasco:hasTotalRecordedMessages  "0" ;
        hasco:hasWebDocument            "https://www.safercare.vic.gov.au/best-practice-improvement/clinical-guidance/neonatal/peripheral-intravenous-iv-catheter-inserti" ;
        hasco:hascoType                 "vstoi:ResponseOption" ;
        vstoi:hasContent                "pmsr:/Neonatal Leg Saphenous vein at the knee" ;
        vstoi:hasLanguage               "pmsr:/en" ;
        vstoi:hasStatus                 "UNPROCESSED" ;
        vstoi:hasVersion                "pmsr:/1" .

